success: true
exit_code: 0
----- stdout -----
Soft-reboot from the hidden session back to the plain decoy

Usage: nails deactivate [OPTIONS]

Options:
  -c, --config <PATH>
          Path to the configuration file (overrides binary-relative discovery)

  -y, --yes
          Proceed without the confirmation prompt

          Deactivation ends the hidden session. Without this flag it prompts on a terminal and refuses to run without one.

      --poweroff
          Power the machine off instead of returning to the decoy.

          Powering off lets RAM decay; leave the machine off for a few minutes before handing it over.

  -v, --verbose...
          More diagnostics on stderr: -v for debug, -vv for trace

  -q, --quiet
          Print only errors and the final result

      --color <WHEN>
          When to use colour

          Possible values:
          - auto:   Colour a stream only when it is a terminal, TERM is not `dumb` and NO_COLOR is unset or empty
          - always: Always colour, even when piped
          - never:  Never colour

          [default: auto]

      --ascii
          Use ASCII markers instead of Unicode symbols and box drawing

      --json
          Print machine-readable JSON on stdout

      --no-log-file
          Do not write the log file on the hidden volume (diagnostics still go to stderr)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

WHAT HAPPENS:
  In the hidden session, deactivation mounts the decoy root at /run/nextroot,
  marks the hidden volume for closing and soft-reboots: every hidden program
  is stopped in order, the top of /run is scrubbed of the hidden session, most
  of the network state it set up is put back (the addresses and routes it gave
  the decoy's own devices stay, and so do its nftables tables on a decoy
  without nft), and the decoy starts again at its own login screen (a display
  manager set to log an account in automatically logs it in again, as at
  boot). When the return cannot undo a link, MAC address, policy-routing rule
  or nftables table the session made or changed (a Wi-Fi interface it added on
  a decoy without iw among them, iwd's own in place of the decoy's included),
  the machine powers off instead. The scrub also deletes the runtime journal,
  which the decoy shares with the hidden session, so a volatile decoy's
  journal starts again at the return. The hidden volume is unmounted and its
  encryption mapping closed by the switch itself. Deactivation warns when the
  return will find no ip or nft, or no iw on a machine with Wi-Fi.

EXAMPLES:
  nails deactivate                    Soft-reboot back to the decoy
  nails deactivate --poweroff         Power off instead
  nails deactivate -y                 Skip the confirmation prompt

----- stderr -----
